=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-beliefs-043] The Biggest Barriers to AI Progress Are Governance Failures, Not Technical Limits: False Dilemma — The node frames the barrier to AI progress as governance failures versus technical limits, implying these are mutually exclusive primary causes and obscuring that both can operate simultaneously as significant constraints.
- [acc-intentions-055] Integrate AI Into Human Work as a Capability Enhancer, Not a Replacement: Optimism Bias — The node consistently foregrounds the productivity and capability gains of human-AI collaboration while underweighting well-documented risks such as automation bias, skill atrophy, accountability diffusion, and uneven distribution of benefits.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Nirvana Fallacy — The claim implicitly compares real-world centralized planning (with its genuine inefficiencies) against an idealized decentralized market rather than against actually-existing market structures, which themselves exhibit oligopolistic concentration in AI.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Hasty Generalization — Extrapolating from open-source successes in software (Linux, Apache) to structural superiority across all AI development contexts — including safety-critical frontier research — is a generalization unsupported by systematic cross-domain evidence.
- [acc-desires-035] The Moral Duty to Create Superhuman Intelligence: Naturalistic Fallacy — The node derives a moral obligation ('cosmic duty') from a descriptive claim about the universe's evolutionary trajectory, illicitly moving from 'is' to 'ought' without ethical justification.
- [acc-desires-035] The Moral Duty to Create Superhuman Intelligence: Appeal to Nature — Framing AI development as part of 'natural evolution' implicitly presents it as inherently good or obligatory, when naturalness alone carries no normative force.
- [acc-desires-028] Accelerating Core AI Capabilities: appeal to consequences — The desire presupposes overwhelmingly positive outcomes from acceleration, selectively foregrounding benefits (medical breakthroughs, productivity) while systematically discounting or ignoring catastrophic downside scenarios.
- [acc-intentions-076] Mandating Dedicated Clean Energy Additionality for High-Density Computing Facilities: Single Cause Fallacy — Designating physical infrastructure as the 'primary prerequisite' oversimplifies the multi-causal nature of AI advancement; talent pipelines, algorithmic breakthroughs, and data availability are equally foundational bottlenecks that the framing marginalizes.
- [acc-beliefs-079] First-Mover Loss from Safety-Induced Deployment Delays: Appeal to Consequences — The node argues that because safety delays produce bad competitive outcomes (market share loss, stagnation), therefore extensive pre-deployment safeguards are unjustified — conflating the undesirability of an outcome with an argument about its causal necessity.
- [acc-beliefs-079] First-Mover Loss from Safety-Induced Deployment Delays: False Dilemma — The claim frames the choice as binary: deploy quickly (win market share) or delay for safety (suffer catastrophic loss), ignoring iterative deployment strategies, staged rollouts, or the possibility that safety investment itself is a competitive differentiator.

